# Lab 7: Detection & Posture Monitoring (Sentinel + Log Analytics)

## Scenario

Following work on detection and posture management, the goal was to build a working pipeline: generate logs from an existing Azure resource, route them into a Log Analytics Workspace, and configure Microsoft Sentinel to detect and alert on specific events automatically, closing the loop from "data exists" to "something tells me without me having to look."

## The design

- Create a Log Analytics Workspace (LAW)
- Enable Diagnostic Settings on an existing storage account to send data-plane logs (blob reads/writes) into the LAW
- Enable Diagnostic Settings at the subscription level to send Activity Log (control-plane events: resource creation, deletion, policy denials) into the same LAW
- Enable Microsoft Sentinel on top of the LAW
- Write a KQL query identifying failed operations, and turn it into a scheduled Analytics Rule that raises an Incident automatically

## What actually happened

This lab surfaced five separate real-world obstacles across two sessions, none of which were part of the original plan.

**1. ProjectCode tagging policy, applied at resource group level.**
Initial resource group creation was denied by `Deny-ProjectCode-Tg-Compliance`. Root cause: the policy checks the tag on the resource group object itself, not on resources created inside it — these are separate taggable objects in Azure. Reused an existing compliant resource group (`rg-creditcards-prod-uks`) to work around this initially.

**2. Storage account network lockdown.**
Attempting to upload/browse blobs in `creditcardstoragelab` returned a 403. Root cause: the account had public network access fully **disabled** (not just IP-restricted), a deliberate private-endpoint-only design from an earlier lab. The correct fix is a Private Endpoint; temporarily re-enabled public access instead, for lab expediency.

**3. Management-group-level security baseline policy.**
The re-enable attempt was itself denied by a separate, higher-scoped policy (`Storage accounts should disable public network access`), enforced at the landing-zone management group, above the subscription, overriding the resource-level setting. Pivoted to using Activity Log (control-plane data) instead of storage data-plane logs, since the policy denial itself was genuinely useful log data — this pivot shaped the rest of the lab.

**4. No tag field for the Sentinel onboarding resource type.**
Enabling Sentinel via the portal's "Add Microsoft Sentinel to a workspace" wizard was denied by the same ProjectCode policy, but this wizard has no Tags step at all, unlike most Azure resource-creation flows. Worked around this using an ARM template deployed via Azure CLI (`az deployment group create`), specifying `tags` explicitly in the template. Created a new resource group and workspace (`rg-lab7-sentinel-clean`, `law-lab7-sentinel-v2`), tagged correctly from creation, and deployed the `Microsoft.OperationsManagement/solutions` resource directly.

